@using Orbit.Web.Services
@inject Translations T

@* What a page shows while it is waiting for its own contents. The same mark and the same turning ring
   the screen before Blazor started carries (see the .app-boot markup in wwwroot/index.html), so waiting
   for the app and waiting inside it look like one thing rather than two.

   It used to be the word "Loading…" alone, on twenty-three screens - which reads as a page that has
   stopped rather than one that is coming, and is exactly the reading the boot screen was rewritten to
   avoid. The word is kept for a screen reader, which has nothing to read in a spinning ring. *@
<div class="loading-orbit @(IsInline ? "loading-orbit-inline" : null)" role="status">
    @* The mark, and the name beside it where the screen is the whole window rather than a corner of one
       - the boot screen's own lockup, so a wait that starts before Blazor and one that starts after it
       look like the same wait. *@
    <span class="loading-orbit-lockup">
        <svg class="loading-orbit-mark" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 26 26" aria-hidden="true">
            <circle cx="13" cy="13" r="3" fill="var(--accent)" />
            <ellipse cx="13" cy="13" rx="11" ry="5.5" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)"
                     stroke-width="1.6" transform="rotate(-28 13 13)" />
        </svg>
        @if (ShowsName)
        {
            <span class="loading-orbit-name">Orbit</span>
        }
    </span>
    <span class="loading-orbit-spinner" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    @* What is being waited for, when the screen knows something more useful than "loading". Under the
       ring and small, because it is the least of the three things here: the mark says whose screen this
       is, the ring says it is still going, and this says what it is going about. *@
    @if (Says is { Length: > 0 } line)
    {
        <span class="loading-orbit-says">@line</span>
    }
    else
    {
        <span class="visually-hidden">@T["Loading…"]</span>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Smaller and in a row, for somewhere that is a corner of a screen rather than the whole of one -
    /// the notifications panel, the column of messages in a conversation. The page-sized version stacks
    /// and takes room of its own, which is what a screen with nothing else on it yet wants.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool IsInline { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Draws the wordmark beside the mark, the way the boot screen does. For a wait that has the whole
    /// window - checking what this account may do, before any page has been drawn - rather than for one
    /// happening inside a page that is already there and already says Orbit at the top.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowsName { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What is being waited for, in a line under the ring. Left unset by a screen with nothing more
    /// useful to say than "loading", which is most of them - and then the word is kept for a screen
    /// reader instead, which has nothing to read in a spinning ring.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Says { get; set; }
}
